%[text] ## Background of Garpinger's trade-off plots
%[text] The design of PID controllers often involves balancing competing objectives, such as minimizing control error and ensuring robustness against disturbances and model uncertainties. The Garpinger trade-off plots provide a powerful visualization of these trade-offs by representing performance and robustness criteria explicitly \[1\]. These plots help to identify optimal PID parameters by highlighting the compromise between error minimization and robustness. The key criteria performance and robustness are typically used in these analyses.
%%
%[text] ### Performance Criteria:$IE$ and $IAE$
%[text] The $\\textrm{IE}${"editStyle":"visual"} criterion measures the integral of the control error $e${"editStyle":"visual"}, i.e., $IE = \\int\_{0}^{\\infty} e(t) \\, \\mathrm{dt}$. This metric captures the overall magnitude of the error but does not emphasize short-term or large deviations.
%[text] The $\\textrm{IAE}${"editStyle":"visual"} criterion improves upon the $\\textrm{IE}${"editStyle":"visual"} by emphasizing absolute deviations, which are often more relevant in practical systems,
%[text] $ IAE = \\int\_{0}^{\\infty} |e(t)| \\, \\mathrm{dt}.$
%[text] The $\\textrm{IAE}${"editStyle":"visual"} is widely used as a performance metric because it penalizes persistent deviations more effectively than the $\\textrm{IE}${"editStyle":"visual"}. A lower $\\textrm{IAE}${"editStyle":"visual"} indicates better performance in terms of setpoint tracking and disturbance rejection. If $|IE|$ and $\\textrm{IAE}${"editStyle":"visual"} yield identical values, no overshoot occurs in the system.
%[text] The computation of the $I\\left(A\\right)E${"editStyle":"visual"} values is typically done for two experiments: i) a step response from the disturbance $d${"editStyle":"visual"} to the $y${"editStyle":"visual"} (disturbance rejection), and ii) a step response from reference $r$ to output$y${"editStyle":"visual"} (setpoint tracking).
%%
%[text] ### Robustness Criterion: Maximum Sensitivity $M\_\\mathrm{st}$
%[text] The robustness of a control system is commonly evaluated using the maximum sensitivity criterion, defined as
%[text] $M\_\\mathrm{st} = \\max\_{\\omega} \\left( \\left| S(\\mathrm{j}\\omega) \\right|, \\left| T(\\mathrm{j}\\omega) \\right| \\right).$
%[text] $S(\\mathrm{j}\\omega)$ is the sensitivity function, representing the system's response to disturbances and model uncertainties at different frequencies. $T(\\mathrm{j}\\omega)$ is the complementary sensitivity function and represents the closed-loop frequency response for setpoint tracking, describing how the output $y${"editStyle":"visual"} responds to changes in the setpoint $r${"editStyle":"visual"}. 
%[text] A lower $M\_\\mathrm{st}$ corresponds to a more robust system that tolerates model variations better. A higher $M\_\\mathrm{st}$ suggests the system is less robust, as uncertainties are amplified more significantly.
%%
%[text] ### The Trade-Offs in Garpinger Plots
%[text] The Garpinger trade-off plots visualize the interplay between performance $\\textrm{IAE}${"editStyle":"visual"} and robustness $M\_\\mathrm{st}$.
%[text] Unstable $K\_\\mathrm{P}$-$K\_\\mathrm{I}$ parameterizations are colored grey (by the term unstable, a closed-loop system that is not internally stable is ment). Constant $M\_\\mathrm{st}$ values are plotted as red line, and constant $\\textrm{IAE}${"editStyle":"visual"} values as blue line. The $\\textrm{IE}${"editStyle":"visual"} value can be calculated by reading the controller gain on the ordinate axis: $IE = -1/K\_\\mathrm{I}$. Where the $|IE|$ value coincides with the horizontal line of the $\\textrm{IAE}${"editStyle":"visual"} value, $|IE|$ and $\\textrm{IAE}${"editStyle":"visual"} have the same value. The $\\textrm{IAE}${"editStyle":"visual"} value does not change along the blue lines. Each point corresponds to a specific set of PI controller gains.
%[text] The optimal line (green in Garpinger's trade-off plot), or Pareto front, is the set of points where no further improvement in one criterion can be achieved without degrading the other and is plotted as green line. Designers can choose parameters along this (green) line depending on the specific application requirements. 
% Plant
plant = tf(2.222, [0.198 1], 'IoDelay', 0.087);

% Controller gains
kp = 2.1;
ki = 13;
kd = 0;
resolutionSpec = "medium"; %[control:dropdown:55d6]{"position":[18,26]}

% Computes stability, maximum robustness and IAE matrices
data = calculateTradeoff(plant, kp, ki, kd, ... %[output:group:3845cd03] %[output:8c626871]
     "ResolutionSpec", resolutionSpec); %[output:group:3845cd03] %[output:8c626871]
%%
% Visualize Garpinger's trade-off plots
numberOfPerformanceLines = 3; %[control:dropdown:6d90]{"position":[28,29]}
robustnessLevels = str2num("[1.1, 1.2, 1.4, 1.6, 2.0, 2.5]"); %[control:dropdown:43ac]{"position":[28,60]}
upsampleScale = "low"; %[control:dropdown:748e]{"position":[17,22]}
externalFigureWindow = false; %[control:dropdown:2d2b]{"position":[24,29]}
smoothParetoFront.method = "movmean"; %[control:dropdown:1fdc]{"position":[28,37]}
smoothParetoFront.window = 5; %[control:editfield:0651]{"position":[28,29]}
plotTradeoff(data, ... %[output:group:5825504d] %[output:97ba7855] %[output:945623d0]
    "NumberOfPerformanceLines", numberOfPerformanceLines, ... %[output:97ba7855] %[output:945623d0]
    "RobustnessLevels", robustnessLevels, ... %[output:97ba7855] %[output:945623d0]
    "UpsampleScale", upsampleScale, ... %[output:97ba7855] %[output:945623d0]
    "ExternalFigureWindow", externalFigureWindow, ... %[output:97ba7855] %[output:945623d0]
    "SmoothParetoFrontMethod", smoothParetoFront.method, ... %[output:97ba7855] %[output:945623d0]
    "SmoothParetoFrontWindow", smoothParetoFront.window); %[output:group:5825504d] %[output:97ba7855] %[output:945623d0]
%[text] Control gains to the left of the Pareto front lead to an overshoot (since $|\\mathrm{IE}| \\neq \\mathrm{IAE}$), to the right of the Pareto front to a less robust system (since $\\textrm{IAE}${"editStyle":"visual"} is constant but $M\_\\mathrm{st}$ increases) and longer settling time (since $\\textrm{IAE}${"editStyle":"visual"} is constant but proportional controller $K\_\\mathrm{P}$ gain is increasing). By using these plots, it is possible to systematically select PID parameters that balance performance and robustness in a way that aligns with the specific needs of the control system. This approach not only improves system reliability but also provides a clear methodology for achieving optimal tuning. 
%[text] The plots show how reducing the $|\\mathrm{IE}|$ or $\\textrm{IAE}${"editStyle":"visual"} (better performance) often comes at the expense of increased $M\_\\mathrm{st}$ (reduced robustness). Lowering the $\\textrm{IAE}${"editStyle":"visual"} typically requires higher PID gains, which may improve disturbance rejection or setpoint tracking but also makes the system more sensitive to noise and model uncertainties. Reducing $M\_\\mathrm{st}$ enhances robustness but may result in slower responses and larger errors. In the Garpinger's trade-off plots, an optimal line emerges, representing the best compromise between performance and robustness (see green line). 
%[text] *Remark*: This optimal line yields different sets of controller parameters when considering either disturbance rejection or setpoint tracking trade-off plots. In some of the Garpinger plots, two lines describing the optimal controller parameters are shown. The green one is related to the disturbance rejection, whereas the magenta one is related to setpoint tracking.
%%
%[text] ### References
%[text] \[1\] O. Garpinger, T. Hägglund, and K. Aström, “Performance and robustness trade-offs in PID control,” Journal of Process Control, vol. 24, 05 2014.

